WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.870
Every action on a device leaves a trace.

2
00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:10.290
A file opened, a message sent, a login at three in the morning: each one is written down somewhere.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:16.930
Long before computers, Kuwait's trackers, the qassas al-athar, read footprints in the sand.

4
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.470
From a single print they could tell who had passed, how fast, and how long ago.

5
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.670
Digital forensics works the same way.

6
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.270
It begins with preservation: the examiner never works on the original.

7
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.370
A write blocker stops any change, and a bit for bit copy is taken.

8
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:40.650
Then comes verification. A cryptographic hash fingerprints the copy.

9
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:46.670
If a single bit changes, the fingerprint changes, and the evidence can no longer be trusted.

10
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:53.330
Every handover is written into the chain of custody: who held the evidence, when, and why.

11
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.990
Without it, nothing stands in court.

12
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.170
Then the reading begins.

13
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:03.230
File systems, memory, logs and network traffic are placed on one timeline,

14
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.150
until the story of what happened appears in order.

15
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.890
Finally, the report: clear, repeatable, and honest about its limits.

16
00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:18.028
Qassas teaches this craft, step by step, in Arabic and English.
